/**
 * @page grammar_enumeration Grammar enumeration
 *
 * @c operon_enum is Operon's exhaustive symbolic-regression entry point. Unlike
 * GP and NSGA-II, it has no population, generations, variation, selection, or
 * reinsertion. It constructs every distinct expression reachable from a
 * restricted @c Grammar up to a structural-complexity limit, fits each novel
 * complete expression, scores it with the selected evaluator, and retains the
 * best results.
 *
 * @section enumeration-flow Construction and ranking
 *
 *
 * The engine processes budgets from 1 through @c MaxComplexity. Complexity is
 * the count of every non-Constant node: variables and operators count, while
 * free coefficient and bias leaves do not. At every nonterminal and budget it
 * canonicalizes candidates with @c Reduce and @c Simplify, then deduplicates by
 * content hash. A complete @c Expression is fitted and scored only when its
 * canonical form is first inserted; intermediate grammar categories are never
 * models by themselves.
 *
 * @section enumeration-language Restricted language
 *
 * @c Grammar is a separate vocabulary from @c PrimitiveSet. It generates a
 * weighted sum of products of recurring factors. A factor is an input variable
 * or one enabled unary wrap around a shallow sub-expression. The current unary
 * wraps are @c Log, @c Exp, @c Sin, @c Sqrt, and @c Cbrt when enabled by the
 * problem's primitive-set configuration.
 *
 * This is deliberately not the full primitive set. The grammar does not
 * generate @c Sub, @c Div, @c Pow, @c Cos, @c Square, @c Aq, @c Tan, or
 * @c Tanh. In particular, enabling a primitive makes it available to normal
 * tree generation and execution, but does not necessarily make it reachable by
 * enumeration. Unary arguments use a non-recursive sub-grammar, so expressions
 * such as @c log(sin(x)) are excluded.
 *
 * @section enumeration-fitting Fitting and objective
 *
 * Each novel complete expression is fitted with a Levenberg-Marquardt
 * @c CoefficientOptimizer before it is ranked. The optimizer uses execution
 * residuals and Jacobians; the evaluator independently applies the requested
 * @c --objective and determines the reported fitness. @c operon_enum uses 50
 * fitting iterations when @c --iterations is zero, because an unfit weighted
 * expression is not a meaningful enumeration result.
 *
 * @section enumeration-cli CLI contract
 *
 * @c operon_enum accepts the dataset, target, train/test ranges, input columns,
 * primitive enable/disable flags, objective, linear-scaling and non-finite
 * policy, seed, @c --iterations, @c --max-complexity, and @c --top-k. It
 * reports progress after each completed complexity budget and prints the best
 * models as fitness plus round-trippable infix expressions.
 *
 * The shared CLI parser also displays many GP-specific options. Those options
 * are inert for @c operon_enum; enumeration has no population or variation
 * controls. @c --top-k must be at least one. The algorithm is single-shot:
 * construct a new @c GrammarEnumerationAlgorithm for a new run rather than
 * reusing one whose buckets have already been built.
 *
 * @section enumeration-api Library API
 *
 * Library callers construct @c Grammar from a primitive configuration and input
 * hashes, then pass it with @c EnumerationConfig, an optimizer, an evaluator,
 * and an RNG to @c GrammarEnumerationAlgorithm. @c Run accepts a callback once
 * each budget is complete; returning true requests an early stop. @c BestTrees
 * exposes the ascending-fitness result set capped by @c TopK.
 */
